 ## Background
-The concept of multi-stage training in 3D Gaussian Splatting emerged as a response to the limitations of single-stage optimization, particularly in complex scenes or when dealing with sparse inputs. Early implementations focused on coarse-to-fine strategies to stabilize geometry initialization [7173-hybrid-coarse-to-fine-strategy.md]. Subsequent work introduced pose-free training pipelines that decouple pose estimation from Gaussian attribute refinement to prevent error propagation [15018-multi-stage-training-with-pose-prediction.md]. Curriculum learning approaches were adapted from volumetric rendering to handle shear-warp pipelines [12921-curriculum-learning-with-slice-and-voxel-supervision.md]. More recent methods have integrated structure-aware densification and interleaved spatio-temporal updates to accelerate convergence and handle dynamic scenes [22986-training-loop-with-structure-aware-densification.md, S2]. These methods are widely used in novel view synthesis, 3D reconstruction from sparse views, and dynamic scene modeling.
